.TH NBSP 1 "July 29, 2026" "nbsp 0.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
nbsp \- a native prompt backend and opinionated Zsh prompt
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nbsp init zsh
[
.B --detached
]
[
.B --autosuggest
]
.br
.B nbsp data
[
.B --status
.I N
]
[
.B --duration-ms
.I N
]
[
.B --jobs
.I N
]
[
.B --format
.I lines|nul
]
.br
.B nbsp refresh
[
.B --cwd
.I PATH
]
[
.B --notify
]
[
.B --force
]
.br
.B nbsp dirs
[
.B --cwd
.I PATH
]
[
.B --format nul
]
.br
.B nbsp cache clear
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nbsp
collects prompt data for macOS and Zsh without spawning Git or Node on the
foreground rendering path. That path still reads the current directory,
repository metadata, the
.B NVM_BIN
environment variable, and a validated status cache. It exposes those facts
through a detached Zsh integration and the
.B data
command. The default
.B init zsh
mode installs an opinionated one-line Zsh prompt that reads
.B NBSP_DATA
(the same data path as detached mode). Detached mode omits that callback so you
own
.B PROMPT
and
.BR RPROMPT .
.PP
The opinionated Zsh callback shows a shortened working directory, Git status,
a version derived from
.BR NVM_BIN ,
command duration, background job count, and exact nonzero exit status.
Failures appear as a compact red status joined to the prompt character, such as
e1% or e127%. Its presentation is intentionally fixed and lives in ordinary
Zsh you can copy from the documentation.
.PP
The
.B data
command obtains the current directory from
.BR getcwd (3),
using its physical absolute pathname rather than the logical spelling in
.BR PWD .
If that call fails, it returns status 1 without writing data output.
The Zsh integration falls back to a minimal
.RB `` >\  ''
prompt when a data frame cannot be loaded in opinionated mode.
Collection reads the branch from resolved Git metadata and consumes a
validated cached status snapshot. A background refresh obtains porcelain-v2
status and asks ZLE to redraw the prompt when ready.
.SH SETUP
Put the install prefix on
.B PATH
before running
.B nbsp init
(unless you invoke the binary by absolute path), then add the following in
.IR ~/.zshrc :
.PP
.EX
export PATH="$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH"
eval "$(nbsp init zsh)"
.EE
.PP
.B init
embeds
.B _NBSP_BIN
as the absolute path of the generating executable so data and refresh hooks
keep working if
.B PATH
is rearranged later. Re-run
.B init
after moving or upgrading the install location.
.PP
To use the backend for a custom prompt instead, use:
.PP
.EX
eval "$(nbsp init zsh --detached)"
.EE
.PP
Add bounded history and one-level directory ghost text to either mode with:
.PP
.EX
eval "$(nbsp init zsh --autosuggest)"
.EE
.PP
.B nbsp
reads
.B NVM_BIN
during each collection, so the integration does not have to follow NVM
initialization. The Node segment reflects the environment when
.B nbsp
runs.
.SH EXTERNAL COMMAND EDITING
The integration defines the private
.B nbsp-edit-command-line
widget and does not replace a public
.B edit-command-line
widget. In each of the Emacs, Vi insert, and Vi command keymaps, it binds
.B Alt+E
only when that key is undefined. Save and quit the editor to return the edited
text to Zsh. The command is not executed until Enter is pressed, and multiline
buffers are supported.
.PP
The editor is selected first from the
.B :zle:edit-command-line
.B editor
zstyle, then from
.BR VISUAL ,
then
.BR EDITOR ,
with
.B vi
as the fallback. For a whole-buffer edit with vi, vim, or nvim, the Zsh cursor
returns to the line and character where the editor was closed. Other editors,
or an absent or invalid cursor report, place it at the end.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B init zsh
Print Zsh integration code. The default installs the fixed, opinionated
prompt. With
.BR --detached ,
install the same data and redraw lifecycle without modifying the prompt.
With
.BR --autosuggest ,
also install the optional autosuggestion engine. It searches in-memory history
for ordinary commands. Safely parseable
.B cd
paths use a bounded asynchronous parent-directory snapshot as the candidate
set, with history used only to rank names in that point-in-time snapshot.
.TP
.B data
Print versioned foreground-collected prompt facts for Zsh and other consumers.
The default
.B lines
format is a versioned sequence of percent-encoded key=value records.
.B --format nul
prints alternating raw key and value records separated by NUL bytes.
Neither format starts Git or Node, but collection still reads the current
directory, repository metadata, environment, and validated cache.
.TP
.B refresh
Perform one synchronous Git snapshot worker operation for a directory. The Zsh
integration launches this command asynchronously.
The
.B --force
option bypasses only the 250 ms duplicate-refresh debounce; locking, timeout,
and pathname-replacement cache publication are unchanged.
.TP
.B cache clear
Snapshot matching cache and temporary names, then remove secure regular files
named with the per-repository hexadecimal prefix and either
.B .cache
or
.BR .cache.tmp.* .
Persistent per-repository lock files are left in place. A concurrent refresh
can lose its temporary file and fail publication, while the preserved lock
inode keeps later refreshes synchronized.
.TP
.B dirs
Emit the internal NUL-framed snapshot of immediate child directories used by
autosuggestions. The snapshot is published only with a completion marker and
is limited to 50 ms, 1,024 entries, and 64 KiB. Nested path suggestions invoke
this command only for the typed parent; it never recursively traverses from the
working directory. On timeout the process is terminated by
.BR SIGALRM ;
it does not return the ordinary operational-failure status.
.SH PROMPT BACKEND
The detached Zsh integration publishes a global associative array named
.B NBSP_DATA
after each successfully parsed, complete schema-2 frame at precmd and after an
asynchronous refresh notification for the same current directory. Values can
be unchanged, including when a failed refresh leaves the previous valid cache
in place. It also exposes
.B nbsp_data_update_functions
for custom redraw callbacks and
.B nbsp_prompt_quote
for quoting dynamic text for the current
.BR PROMPT_PERCENT ,
.BR PROMPT_SUBST ,
and
.B PROMPT_BANG
settings without changing those options.
.PP
The complete schema, lifecycle, safe parsing rules, and custom prompt examples
are
documented in
.IR detached-mode/data-lifecycle.mdx ,
installed under the nobsprompt documentation directory.
.SH GIT COLLECTION
Foreground collection reads the current branch or abbreviated detached object
directly from
.B HEAD
under the resolved Git directory. The background worker removes
repository-selecting Git environment variables and invokes:
.PP
.EX
git --no-optional-locks -C ROOT --git-dir=GIT_DIR --work-tree=ROOT status --porcelain=v2 --branch --show-stash --untracked-files=normal --ignore-submodules=dirty --no-renames
.EE
.PP
The counters represent porcelain records. With normal untracked handling, an
untracked directory can be one record. Submodule worktree dirt is ignored and
rename detection is disabled.
.PP
After collection, the worker reads
.B HEAD
again and publishes only when its branch matches the porcelain branch.
Foreground collection uses cached counters only when the cached branch matches
the current direct
.B HEAD
value. This prevents mixed branch and counter snapshots; a branch can appear
with an ellipsis while no coherent counter snapshot is available.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Presentation is not configurable in built-in mode. Use detached mode to choose
colors, symbols, layout, segment visibility, or duration thresholds.
.TP
.B NBSP_GIT_TIMEOUT_MS
Background Git timeout in milliseconds. Values from 50 through 60,000 are
accepted; an absent or invalid value uses 1,500.
.TP
.B NBSP_CACHE_DIR
Select the cache root when the variable is set. It must be an absolute path; an
invalid or insecure override fails closed instead of falling back. Cache facts
then remain unavailable to prompt collection, while cache mutation commands
fail. When it is unset, an absolute
.B XDG_CACHE_HOME
selects
.BR $XDG_CACHE_HOME/nbsp .
A relative XDG value is ignored, and the fallback is
.BR $HOME/Library/Caches/nbsp .
.PP
The cache root and its
.B git
subdirectory must be owned by the current user with mode 0700. Cache,
temporary, and lock artifacts must be owned, single-link regular files with
mode 0600. Symlink path components are rejected apart from the exact macOS
system aliases for
.B /tmp
and
.BR /var .
Status publication fsyncs a complete temporary file and renames it over the
cache pathname. This is atomic pathname replacement, not a guarantee that the
directory entry survives sudden power loss.
.TP
.B NBSP_AUTOSUGGEST_HIGHLIGHT_STYLE
ZLE region-highlight style for optional ghost text. Default: fg=8.
.SH AUTOSUGGESTIONS
The optional engine captures up to 4,096 nonempty commands while examining at
most 8,192 recent history event numbers at a precmd boundary. Ordinary commands
use the newest exact, case-sensitive prefix from that snapshot. For one
supported static operand, the names in one bounded, point-in-time
parent-directory snapshot define the candidate set. A child can disappear
before acceptance. One matching name is offered directly; when several match,
history may rank only a name present in that snapshot. History paths absent
from the snapshot are never offered as directory candidates.
.PP
Path-aware suggestions support relative, absolute, ./, ../, ~/, nested,
closed-quoted, and backslash-escaped paths with exactly one nonempty operand.
Variables, substitutions, globs (including
.B ^
and
.B #
under
.BR EXTENDED_GLOB ),
operators, redirections, unmatched quotes, named directories, and multiple
operands remain ordinary history or Tab-completion cases. A failed or
incomplete snapshot produces no directory suggestion.
.PP
Explicit dot, dot-dot,
.BR ./ ,
and
.B ../
operands resolve from the current directory without a
.B cdpath
search. For other relative operands with
.B POSIX_CD
off, the current directory is scanned when
.B cdpath
(the special array corresponding to
.BR CDPATH )
has no dot or empty entry, or when such an entry is first. With
.B POSIX_CD
on, it is scanned when
.B cdpath
is empty or its first entry is dot or empty. Otherwise the engine cannot
guarantee that the current directory is Zsh's first search root and leaves the
input to ordinary history or completion.
.PP
Right Arrow accepts a suggestion in native Emacs and Vi insert keymaps only
when its binding was the standard
.B forward-char
or
.B vi-forward-char
widget. Custom bindings and foreign ZLE display text are preserved. Tab remains
Zsh completion and does not accept nbsp ghost text. The
.B nbsp-autosuggest-accept
and
.B nbsp-autosuggest-toggle
widgets may be bound explicitly. If zsh-autosuggestions is present, nbsp skips
or disables its own engine rather than running both. A conflict present at
initialization does not set the initialization guard, so initialization may be
repeated after removing it. An engine detected later produces one warning at
the next precmd boundary.
.SH GIT MARKERS
.B +
staged,
.B ~
modified,
.B ?
untracked,
.B !
conflicted,
.B ^
ahead,
.B v
behind, and
.B *
stashes.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns 0 on success and on benign refresh no-ops, including a non-repository
directory, debounce, or a lock already held by another refresh. Returns 2 for
invalid command-line usage and 1 for operational, serialization, cache, or
non-timeout Git failures. A Git timeout from
.B refresh
returns 124.
.PP
A
.B dirs
timeout terminates that process with
.B SIGALRM
rather than returning 1. Optional missing prompt context, such as an absent or
invalid status cache, does not by itself prevent prompt rendering.
